I read your version closely. It should run, but it brings back the problems from before. It is also missing the two things you asked for in the last round.

Missing from your version

The summary is counts only. WriteSummary(ws, nDiff, nOutlines) writes “Changed cells: n” and “Red bar outlines: n”. That is the “tells me there are x red cells” summary you rejected. There is no list of Add / Change / Delete with seiban, worker and date.
There is no “Changed” column with a circle on the changed rows, so you cannot filter to the rows that changed.

Problems in the code
3. The old sheet is still copied across workbooks (wsOldSrc.Copy After:=…). That is the heavy step, and I think it caused the “press twice” behaviour. If anything fails after the copy, the stray sheet stays, because ErrHandler does not delete the Compare_* sheets.
4. A popup blocks the run. MsgBox "Copying sheets (please wait)..." stops the macro until you click OK.
5. CompareCells can give wrong results.

Error cells: it uses On Error Resume Next around CStr(...). If a cell holds an error value such as #N/A, oldVal and newVal keep the previous cell’s text, so a difference can be reported or missed wrongly.
Array bounds: it loops to Max(dOld.LastRow, dNew.LastRow) but chgOld and chgNew are sized by their own sheet. If the two sheets have different sizes, you get “Subscript out of range”.
Cell-by-cell reads: it reads about 500,000 cells one at a time. The array read I used does the same in about a second.
Position-based matching: it matches by row and column position. If a worker row is inserted anywhere, every row below it shows as changed. My version matches by company and name, and by date.
It compares the whole sheet, including the header rows and columns A to N, not just the date cells.
LoadSheetData takes the last row from column B (the ID column). I checked your real file: column B ends at row 507, the same as the name column, so it works here. It would break if the last workers had no ID.
The If wsNewSrc Is Nothing check never runs. wbNew.Sheets(sheetName) raises an error when the sheet is missing, so you only see “ERROR: Subscript out of range”.
A minor point: OnAction = "CompareWithOldVersion" works, but the workbook-qualified name is safer in newer Excel.

What I would keep from yours: the sheet picker (InputBox, default 3), if you want to compare sheets other than the task sheet. Also xlThick, if you prefer the thicker frame.

